= Carbon cycle — Technical =

== Stocks and fluxes ==
In a simplified reservoir model for compartment i:

<pre>
dC_i/dt = sum of carbon inflows - sum of carbon outflows
</pre>

The model requires defined compartment boundaries, carbon units and time units. Fluxes measured in mass per year must not be confused with stocks measured in mass. A large gross natural flux can coexist with a much smaller net perturbation that accumulates over time.

== Fast and slow transfers ==
Biological fixation, respiration, decomposition and air–sea exchange operate at shorter characteristic timescales than many geological weathering, burial and tectonic transfers. Reservoir turnover times differ, and no single timescale describes the entire cycle.

== Measurement and uncertainty ==
Carbon inventories require observations of concentrations, biomass, ocean chemistry, fluxes and accounting boundaries. Units can be carbon mass or carbon-dioxide mass: CO2/C molecular mass is approximately 44/12, so the values must be converted before comparison.

